The page is a pure numerical table with no derivation. It continues the table of pp. 13 and 15. It has two blocks: A = 0.400…0.440 on the left and A = 0.440…0.480 on the right, in steps of 0.001.

Each block has five columns:
(1) the argument A;
(2) B(A) to 5 decimals;
(3) the first difference of B, in units of 1e-5, written between rows;
(4) C(A) to 5 decimals;
(5) the first difference of C, in units of 1e-5.

Identification (mine, verified numerically):
- B(A) = log10(1 + 10^(-A)). Examples: A = 0.4 gives 10^-0.4 = 0.398107 and log10(1.398107) = 0.145540. A = 0.44 gives 0.13452. A = 0.48 gives 0.12422. The previous and next pages also fit: A = 0.5 gives 0.11933 and A = 0.32 gives 0.16986.
- Column 4 is C = A + B = log10(1 + 10^A). For example, 0.400 + 0.14554 = 0.54554.
- Since A advances by 0.001 (100 units of 1e-5), the differences satisfy ΔC = 100 − ΔB. On every row, columns 3 and 5 sum to 100 (28+72, 29+71, 27+73, 26+74, 25+75).

Purpose: this is a table of addition (Gaussian) logarithms. Given log a and log b with a ≥ b, set A = log a − log b. Then log(a+b) = log a + B(A) = log b + C(A). Logarithms of sums (and, by inverse entry, differences) can thus be found without returning to the numbers themselves. The values were presumably obtained by computing B at coarse nodes and filling in by differences or interpolation. That is my inference; no working is shown.
